Ah, that makes the picture crystal clear. You have a classic enterprise setup: an ASP.NET Web Forms (.aspx) web application called "Dime", and your actual business data or logic trapped inside legacy .NET Class Library DLLs called "EDP".

Because these are legacy (likely running on standard .NET Framework 4.x), you face a technical roadblock: Modern AI SDKs, including Microsoft Semantic Kernel, are designed for modern .NET 8/10. While Semantic Kernel has a .NET Standard 2.0 bridge, trying to force it directly inside an old .aspx application will break your dependency injection, slow down your IIS app pools, and cause endless NuGet DLL binding redirect nightmares.

The correct, enterprise-grade way to do this is a Sidecar API Architecture.

The Target Architecture
Instead of stuffing Semantic Kernel into your legacy code, you leave "Dime" and "EDP" exactly as they are. You build a tiny, modern .NET 8 Web API that acts as your "AI Engine."

+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|               LEGACY ENVIRONMENT (.NET Framework)           |
|                                                             |
|   Dime (.aspx Web App)  =======>  Calls legacy EDP.dll      |
+---------------------+---------------------------------------+
                      |
              Internal HTTP REST Call
                      |
                      v
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
|               MODERN AI ENVIRONMENT (.NET 8+)               |
|                                                             |
|   New AI Web API <========> Uses Microsoft Semantic Kernel  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
Step-by-Step Implementation Guide
Step 1: Create the .NET 8 AI Web API
Create a brand new, lightweight ASP.NET Core Web API project using .NET 8+. This is where you install Semantic Kernel.

In this new project, install the required packages:

Bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.SemanticKernel
dotnet add package Microsoft.SemanticKernel.Connectors.OpenAI
Configure your Semantic Kernel inside its Program.cs:

C#
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Register Semantic Kernel in DI container
builder.Services.AddTransient(sp =>
{
    var kernelBuilder = Kernel.CreateBuilder();
    
    // Add your LLM provider (Azure OpenAI or OpenAI)
    kernelBuilder.AddAzureOpenAIChatCompletion(
        deploymentName: "your-deployment",
        endpoint: "your-endpoint",
        apiKey: "your-key"
    );
    
    return kernelBuilder.Build();
});

var app = builder.Build();
Step 2: Turn your Legacy EDP functions into "AI Plugins"
Semantic Kernel allows the LLM to call native C# code via Plugins. You want the AI to be able to fetch data using your legacy EDP DLLs.

Create a controller or endpoint in your .NET 8 API that acts as a proxy to fetch data, or if your EDP DLLs are compatible with .NET Standard, reference them directly in the .NET 8 project.

Define a Plugin class in your .NET 8 project:

C#
using System.ComponentModel;
using Microsoft.SemanticKernel;

public class EdpLegacyPlugin
{
    [KernelFunction, Description("Queries the legacy EDP system to get client historical records.")]
    public async Task<string> GetClientData([Description("The unique client identifier")] string clientId)
    {
        // Option A: Call your EDP dll directly if it can be referenced
        // var data = LegacyEdpLibrary.FetchRecord(clientId);
        
        // Option B: Web API calls back to a legacy endpoint you exposed in Dime .aspx
        using var client = new HttpClient();
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync($"https://dime-app/api/legacy-edp?id={clientId}");
        
        return response; 
    }
}
Step 3: Execute the AI Request
Create an endpoint in your .NET 8 API that your .aspx application will hit when a user wants to chat or run an AI prompt.

C#
app.MapPost("/api/chat", async (ChatRequest request, Kernel kernel) =>
{
    // Import your legacy data plugin so the LLM can use it
    kernel.ImportPluginFromObject(new EdpLegacyPlugin(), "EdpPlugin");

    // Ask the kernel to process the user's intent
    var result = await kernel.InvokeAsync("EdpPlugin", "GetClientData", new() {
        { "clientId", request.UserQuery }
    });

    return Results.Ok(new { answer = result.ToString() });
});
Step 4: Wire it up to your Dime .aspx Application
Now, go back to your legacy Web Forms application. When a user clicks a button or interacts with a chat UI in Dime, you use standard, legacy-friendly HttpClient or HttpWebRequest to call your new .NET 8 AI service.

Inside your Default.aspx.cs code-behind:

C#
protected void btnSubmitQuery_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    string userQuery = txtUserQuery.Text;

    // Use standard .NET Framework WebClient/HttpClient to talk to the sidecar
    using (var client = new System.Net.Http.HttpClient())
    {
        var payload = new { UserQuery = userQuery };
        var json = Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.SerializeObject(payload);
        var content = new StringContent(json, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

        // Call the .NET 8 Semantic Kernel Sidecar
        var response = client.PostAsync("http://localhost:5000/api/chat", content).Result;
        if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
        {
            var jsonResult = response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync().Result;
            dynamic result = Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.DeserializeObject(jsonResult);
            
            // Output the AI answer directly to your .aspx literal/label control
            lblAiResponse.Text = result.answer;
        }
    }
}
Why this is the safest route for Legacy Apps
Zero Risk to Production: You don't have to upgrade your legacy application to .NET 8, which could break thousands of lines of old .aspx lifecycle code.

Clean Separation of Concerns: Your AI stack remains modern, easily maintainable, and up-to-date with Microsoft's latest Semantic Kernel releases, while your legacy EDP remains stable and untouched.

Performance: Heavy AI operations (managing chat histories, handling token formatting, orchestrating multiple plugins) happen on a modern .NET 8 runtime, preventing your legacy IIS App Pool from running out of memory.
